For Pantheon’s layered visualizations, I’d combine those three into a rule: version the interpretation, record its uncertainty, and preserve what it was based on.

Principle	What it does
Version control	Commit each interpretation with its parent, inputs, and transformation settings. Branch when two explanations fit the same evidence.
Probability	Attach confidence to individual claims. “Movement in this region” and “this is the shape of a hand” are different claims, even if the same image suggests both. Use numeric probabilities when you have a way to calibrate them.
Immutability	Make the evidence and revision history append-only for as long as they’re retained. New evidence creates a new revision. A hash lets you detect changes to a retained record.

Say a sparse LiDAR capture supports two plausible outlines. Pantheon can show both, let you choose which one to render in front, and keep each outline traceable to the same capture. If a later scan favors one, that changes the assessment in a new revision. The earlier view remains inspectable as the interpretation available at the time.

Layer priority controls what you see first. Evidence controls what you can claim. The interface should let you click any rendered layer and see its source, revision, confidence, and which details the renderer filled in.